<Base title="How Long Does a Water Heater Last? Tank and Tankless" description="Tank water heaters usually last 8 to 12 years and tankless ones 15 to 20. How to find your water heater's age and the warning signs before it leaks.">
  <section>
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › Guides › Water heater lifespan</p>
    <h1>How long does a water heater last?</h1>
    <p class="lede">A tank water heater usually lasts <b>8 to 12 years</b>; a tankless one <b>15 to 20</b>. Hard water shortens both. Start by finding out how old yours is: <a href="/water-heater-age/">decode the serial number</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Warning signs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Rust-coloured hot water, or rust around the base or the fittings on top.</li>
      <li>Water pooling under the tank. A leaking tank cannot be repaired.</li>
      <li>Popping or rumbling while it heats (sediment on the bottom).</li>
      <li>Running out of hot water sooner than it used to.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Making an older tank last</h2>
    <p>Drain a few gallons from the valve at the bottom once a year to clear sediment, and check the anode rod every few years. The anode rod is a metal rod that corrodes so the tank doesn't. Replacing it before it is used up can add years to a tank that isn't leaking.</p>
  </section>
  <p class="note">Most tanks come with a 6-year warranty, some 9 or 12. The warranty length is usually on the label, and it's a fair guide to how long the maker expects the tank to last.</p>
</Base>
